## Why This Is Asked

Assess ability to design production-ready, self-healing inference pipelines, with drift detection and safe rollback in TF Serving.

## Key Concepts

- Drift metrics: KL divergence on input features and model-proxy accuracy/latency proxies
- Canary rollout and automatic rollback
- Per-model baselines, rolling windows, alerting, and audit logs

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudocode for drift check and rollback trigger
function evaluateDrift(model, payload) {
  const dist = klDivergence(payload.features, model.baseline);
  const lat = measureLatency(model);
  const acc = approximateAccuracy(model, payload.labels);
  return (dist > model.driftThreshold) || (lat > model.latencyThreshold) || (acc < model.accuracyLowerBound);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How to set drift thresholds? How to test rollback safely? How to handle cascading rollbacks across models?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests real-world streaming inference design, per-model batching, and robust rollout strategies in TF Serving, including stateful handling for ASR and safe upgrades.

## Key Concepts
- Streaming inference for ASR
- Per-model dynamic batching and isolation
- Stateful routing per session
- Canary rollouts with latency/throughput targets

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example: pseudo streaming buffer
function feedChunk(sessionId, chunk) {
  if (!state[sessionId]) state[sessionId] = [];
  state[sessionId].push(chunk);
  if (state[sessionId].length >= 4) {
    // call inference for the buffered chunks
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate tail latency under burst traffic?
- How would you detect and respond to model drift in streaming inputs?
- How would you extend this to add a third modality (text) with routing?
